#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133518 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad9f55b2-ce37-507f-8cb6-7d4e30e6868a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace"}] :question/id #uuid "b8bbe0e1-8bf1-5b3a-a77e-6350ea77e83f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5575" :question/problem "Verify whether the vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3$ are linearly independent, and find the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace spanned by the vectors $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we verify whether $\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3$ are linearly independent. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{3}↔R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{vmatrix}R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-3)R_{1} \\\\\n&= R_{3}:=R_{3} + (-4)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -10 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -15\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{3}:=R_{3} + (-\\frac{3}{2})R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, the pivot columns correspond to the linearly independent vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ that form the basis of our span.\n\nNow, the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace spanned by the linearly independent columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S} \\: \\mathbf{x} = A(A^T\\!A)^{-1}\\!A^T\\,\\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n$[\\mid \\mid \\mid; a_{1} \\mid a_{2}; \\mid \\mid \\mid]$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{S}x &= \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot (\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix})^{-1} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}26 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{bmatrix}^{-1} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\frac{1}{14}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 26\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133519 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c07721dd-4b97-5d57-8de1-5098117e3071" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Plane"}] :question/id #uuid "ac8363a8-fc77-52e0-b060-5e3a45c5171a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5576" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the plane $\\mathbf{r}=s\\mathbf{v}+t\\mathbf{w}$, where $s,t\\in\\mathbb R$, and\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $\\mathbf v$ and $\\mathbf w$ are linearly independent.\n\nThe orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S} \\: \\mathbf{x} = A\\left(A^T\\!A\\right)^{-1}\\!A^T\\,\\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace\n\n$$\nS=\\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{w}\\}\n$$\n\nin $\\mathbb{R}^3$. In our case, we have\n$[\\mid \\mid \\mid; v \\mid w; \\mid \\mid \\mid]$\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{S}x &= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot (\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix})^{-1} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}12 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 12\\end{bmatrix}^{-1} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\frac{1}{32}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134221 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad9f55b2-ce37-507f-8cb6-7d4e30e6868a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cdd6106-99d9-5541-a7ae-46cafa6cfdef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131769} {:answer/id #uuid "9d78dd9f-8345-5de7-adc6-54711225fd1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131770} {:answer/id #uuid "1110c3eb-0f6d-549e-acb7-1ea49b28760e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131771} {:answer/id #uuid "163e132e-4669-594a-9930-7dc12dcaff6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131772} {:answer/id #uuid "20b8fbcc-ffb8-5bb1-ba18-6a8700e54b37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131773}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1110c3eb-0f6d-549e-acb7-1ea49b28760e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131771} :answer-field/id #uuid "b70ed4bf-2cc2-5089-aa64-7bbf0cfc1b17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131768}] :question/id #uuid "22c16d4d-ef3b-582d-a66e-91e72ba35f45" :question/math-academy-id "q-40570" :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nFind the vector in the subspace spanned by ${a_{1}, a_{2}}$\nthat is closest to the vector $x$, where\n$a_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, a_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}, x = \\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}$." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
